\title{Truth table for implication}
\date{2026-01-28T20:57:19Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{Let's figure out what the truth table for [implication](003S) should be. I like to think of
it this way: if someone makes you a promise of the form "if ... then ...", in what scenarios have they lied to you?}

\p{Consider the propositions
\ul{
  \li{#{P} = "You fulfill all the requirements for an A."}
  \li{#{Q} = "You get an A."}
}
In the syllabus I have in fact promised that the proposition #{P \to Q}
is true. Let's consider every possible scenario; in which scenario(s)
should you be mad at me for lying to you?
\ul{
\li{You complete all the requirements and get an A.  This is great;
  I have kept my promise.}
\li{You complete all the requirements but don't get an A.  Obviously
  in this scenario I lied and you should be super mad at me, and
  probably complain to my department chair or the Provost.}
\li{Let's next consider the scenario where you don't complete the
  requirements for an A, and don't get an A.  You might be mad at
  yourself but you certainly can't be mad at me.  I still made a true
  statement.}
\li{The last case is the one that sometimes trips people up: what if
  you don't complete the requirements, but you get an A anyway?  You
  obviously won't be upset in this scenario (though other students
  might!), but \em{did I lie}?  Actually, I didn't.  I only said
  what would happen if you DO complete the requirements.  I am free to
  do whatever I want (including giving you an A anyway) if you
  don't. (In fact, I occasionally do this in special circumstances!)}
}
}

\p{So overall we have the following truth table:
##{\begin{array}{ccc}
    P & Q & P \to Q \\ \hline
    \True & \True & \True \\
    \True & \False & \False \\
    \False & \True & \True \\
    \False & \False & \True \\
\end{array}}
}

\p{In other words, the only scenario in which an implication #{P \to Q} is
false is when #{P} is true but #{Q} is false. It is true in all other
cases. "If the premise is false, anything goes!"}

